<!-- UFACTORY Lite 6 gripper, WIDE (default) finger mounting, lifted out of the full-arm model as a
     standalone graftable asset. Geometry, inertials, the finger coupling and the actuator are
     VERBATIM from google-deepmind/mujoco_menagerie lite6_gripper_wide.xml, BSD-3-Clause
     (LICENSE.menagerie), checked out at 8161bba (2026-09-04), except where marked below.

     Version, as precisely as it can be pinned. The gripper subtree's CONTENT was finalised by
     PR #89 (merged 15b7a2b, 2024-09-09; assets 7db7b00, models db38690, wide/narrow rename
     27583fe). The only commit to touch the file since is ac6b2b0 (2026-05-18), a repo-wide XML
     reformat that changed line wrapping and not one attribute -- verified by diffing 47b0810..HEAD.
     So 2024-09-09 is the real version of what this file copies, NOT the checkout date, and not
     71f066ad (2026-07-04), which is merely the last commit to touch the ufactory_lite6 DIRECTORY
     and edits scene.xml's <statistic> tags rather than this model.

     Upstream of menagerie there is no version to pin, and the provenance is weaker than
     linear_4310's. UFACTORY publishes the gripper as an unversioned .step on its download page
     (the same page versions its manuals, e.g. "Hardware Manual V2.6.2", but not the CAD). The
     xarm_ros2 URDF that menagerie's README cites supplied the ARM only -- its Lite 6 gripper has
     the fingers FUSED to the body -- and the README links it at `master`, unpinned. The separated
     fingers this file needs came from that .step, hand-split along the finger tracks in Onshape by
     the menagerie contributor. Consequently the <inertial> values below are NOT manufacturer data:
     the README states they were estimated in Onshape by picking a density that reproduces each
     part's real mass and assuming uniform distribution. Treat them as an estimate if a grasp ever
     turns out to be inertia-sensitive.

     Checked against UFACTORY's own published numbers (docs.lite6.ufactory.cc 5.End-Effector, plus
     reseller spec tables for the figures that page omits):

       stroke      the fingers BOLT ON TWO WAYS and the vendor quotes a different window for each:
                   default ("wide") 20-38 mm, reverse ("narrow") 0-16 mm -- one 16 mm stroke, moved
                   outboard or inboard. Measured pad-to-pad on the models: wide 22.24 -> 38.42 mm,
                   narrow 0.02 -> 16.20 mm. The geometry is therefore VERIFIED against the vendor
                   spec, which is the strongest check this asset gets given the hand-split CAD
                   provenance above. THIS FILE IS THE WIDE MOUNTING; the narrow one is the only way
                   to reach anything thinner than 22 mm, and is not currently vendored.
       states      "There are two states of the gripper, open and close." The SDK exposes exactly
                   open_lite6_gripper / close_lite6_gripper / stop_lite6_gripper, and the API's
                   only parameter is op=open|close|stop -- no position, no force. Contrast
                   xarm_set_gripper_position / xarm_set_bio_gripper_pos_force, which UFACTORY does
                   provide for its OTHER grippers. That restriction is preserved by the CATALOG
                   entry, whose open_command and closed_command are one value each, rather than by
                   the actuator type -- see the servo note below.
       force       5 N, per every reseller spec table (Generation Robots, RobotShop, MyBotShop);
                   UFACTORY's own End-Effector page does not state it. The actuator below carries
                   upstream's forcerange of +-10 N, i.e. TWICE the quoted grip force -- left as
                   upstream has it so this file stays a faithful copy, but flagged: a grasp tuned
                   here can hold roughly double what the real gripper would.
       payload     <=600 g (vendor). Modelled mass is 308 g against a quoted 350 g, the 42 g gap
                   being another consequence of the Onshape density estimate.

     Extraction: the upstream file models the whole Lite 6, gripper included, as one tree; this is
     the `gripper_body` subtree of `link6` promoted to worldbody. Upstream mounts it at link6 with
     an identity transform, so the gripper's own frame is already the flange frame -- fingers open
     along +-y, approach along +z, which is the catalog mount convention (as robotiq and yam_jaw
     are), so no adapter body and no mount_quat are needed.

     Aperture. The two slides carry MIRRORED ranges, so the pad gap is their DIFFERENCE, and the
     wide mounting bolts each finger outboard, which adds a constant the joints never see:
     gap = (right - left) + 22.220 mm, verified against mj_geomDistance across the stroke
     (22.240 / 30.220 / 38.420 mm at joint differences 0.020 / 8.000 / 16.200). The catalog entry
     carries that offset; a narrow entry would carry 0.

     Authored departures from the upstream file, and nothing else:
     * the <default class="lite6"> hierarchy is INLINED. Upstream hangs damping/armature, the
       visual and collision geom classes and the site defaults off a childclass declared on
       `link_base` -- an ancestor this subtree does not include. Carried implicitly, the fingers
       would graft with zero damping and zero armature and the collision geoms would gain the mass
       their class zeroes out. Every value below is the one the class would have supplied.
     * the <keyframe> is dropped: its qpos spans the arm this subtree no longer has.
     * upstream's `motor` (ctrl = newtons) becomes a SATURATING position servo, ctrl = the left
       slide's position over its own range, kp=500000, dampratio=1, forcerange UNCHANGED at the
       +-10 N upstream ships. kp is deliberately far beyond anything a real servo would run: it
       exists so kp*error exceeds the forcerange for ANY nonzero error, turning the servo into a
       constant-force clamp that stops exactly at its target. Measured against upstream's motor,
       the grip is identical while the stops are strictly better -- a constant force has nothing to
       stop it at a limit, so the motor parks the jaw 0.6-1.3 mm OUTSIDE the joint range and drags
       the mirroring equality more than a millimetre out of true, and those out-of-range qpos would
       be written into every emitted trajectory. Avoid kp below ~5e4: near kp*error ~ forcerange
       the solver chatters between gripping and releasing.
     * the two <contact><exclude> entries below, which restore filtering upstream gets implicitly.
     * the two tip MESH colliders become BOXES. This one is a bug fix; see the next section.
     * upstream's `end_effector` site is renamed `grasp_site`, pose unchanged. The catalog's
       planner_grasp_site is only added to the emitted spec, not to the model the builder compiles,
       and builder.rest_pose falls back to a site named `grasp_site` to know where the tool is.
       Every other catalog asset ships one under that name (linear_4310's vendor file included);
       named `end_effector`, this one made rest_pose return None, the emitted rest pose fell back
       to all-zero joints, and validate T2 rejected every seed with the gripper body 30 mm inside
       the last arm link.

     Why the tip colliders had to change. As shipped, this gripper does not hold anything: closing
     on a welded block, the jaws sink most of the way through it and develop a fraction of their
     force. Measured on the standalone asset, closing from open onto blocks 24-38 mm thick --

                      as shipped (mesh)              with box colliders        theory
       block 24 mm    qpos -0.107  10.15 N  -0.76    -0.822  10.00 N  -0.07    -0.900
       block 26 mm    qpos -0.013   1.77 N  -1.89    -1.822  10.00 N  -0.07    -1.900
       block 30 mm    qpos -0.014   2.22 N  -3.89    -3.822  10.00 N  -0.07    -3.900
       block 34 mm    qpos -0.015   2.48 N  -5.87    -5.822  10.00 N  -0.07    -5.900
       block 38 mm    qpos -0.017   3.34 N  -7.88    -7.822  10.00 N  -0.07    -7.900
                                    (force, penetration mm; qpos in mm)

     -- the shipped column shows the jaws closing to within 0.1 mm of fully shut no matter how
     thick the block is, penetrating it by up to 7.9 mm and gripping at 1.8-3.3 N of the 10 N
     available. Boxed, qpos tracks the geometric prediction to 0.078 mm at every size and the grip
     is a flat 10.00 N. The boxes match the meshes' world extents to 0.010 mm.

     This is UPSTREAM's behaviour, not something the extraction introduced: the same test on
     menagerie's own full-arm lite6_gripper_wide.xml puts the left finger at +0.725 mm against a
     -0.900 prediction, 1.64 mm into the block. The NARROW model passes the identical test
     (-3.968 against -4.000, 0.00 mm penetration, 9.90 N), which is what makes the wide colliders
     specifically, rather than the test, the thing at fault.

     The cause is contact COUNT, not shape. The tip mesh is a plain 4.40 x 20.00 x 20.00 mm slab,
     so a box is its convex hull rather than a simplification of it -- but mesh-vs-box collision
     yields a single contact point, and one point cannot resist a squeeze, so the solver lets the
     jaw pass through. Box-vs-box generates a face manifold and holds. Upstream splitting each
     finger into base and tip already shows the shape was not the remaining problem. The same move
     is what linear_4310's vendor file makes for its own L-shaped fingers.

     NOT a departure, and deliberately preserved: the wide fingers carry ASYMMETRIC sliding
     friction upstream -- left 6.0, right 5.0, with identical everything else. That reads like a
     slip rather than a design, but it is the shipped model's behaviour and this file is a copy;
     correcting it would silently change grasp outcomes against the reference. Note also that wide
     uses frictionloss 0.4 on the slides where narrow uses 1.0. -->
<mujoco model="lite6_gripper_wide">
  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="assets" autolimits="true"/>

  <!-- upstream's integrator; the generated embodiments this grafts into compile with
       implicitfast anyway, so standalone loads must match -->
  <option integrator="implicitfast"/>

  <asset>
    <material name="white" rgba="1 1 1 1"/>
    <material name="black" rgba=".1 .1 .1 1"/>

    <mesh file="visual/gripper_lite_body.stl"/>
    <mesh file="visual/gripper_lite_left_finger_wide.stl"/>
    <mesh file="visual/gripper_lite_right_finger_wide.stl"/>
    <mesh name="gripper_lite_body_c" file="collision/gripper_lite_body.stl"/>
    <mesh file="collision/gripper_lite_left_finger_wide_base.stl"/>
    <mesh file="collision/gripper_lite_right_finger_wide_base.stl"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <body name="gripper_body" pos="0 0 0" quat="1 0 0 0">
      <inertial pos="0.0 0.0 0.026" quat="1 0 0 0" mass="0.26"
        diaginertia="0.00016117 0.000118 0.00014455"/>
      <geom name="gripper_lite_body" type="mesh" mesh="gripper_lite_body" material="white"
        contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"/>
      <geom name="gripper_lite_body_c" type="mesh" mesh="gripper_lite_body_c"
        group="3" mass="0" density="0"/>
      <!-- Finger and base separated to avoid convex hull preventing gripping -->
      <body name="gripper_left_finger" pos="0 0 0.054" quat="1 0 0 0">
        <inertial pos="0.0 -0.01 0.01" quat="1 0 0 0" mass="0.024"
          diaginertia="1.961e-6 2.274e-6 1.238e-6"/>
        <joint name="gripper_left_finger" pos="0 0 0" axis="0 1 0" type="slide" limited="true"
          range="-0.0081 -1e-5" damping="1" armature="0.1" frictionloss="0.4"/>
        <geom name="gripper_left_finger_visual" type="mesh" mesh="gripper_lite_left_finger_wide"
          material="black" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"/>
        <geom name="gripper_left_finger_base" type="mesh" mesh="gripper_lite_left_finger_wide_base"
          material="black" group="3" mass="0" density="0" condim="1" solimp="2 1 0.01"
          solref="0.01 1" friction="6 0.5 0.1"/>
        <!-- the tip's MESH collider is replaced by the box it already is: the mesh is a plain
             4.40 x 20.00 x 20.00 mm slab, so this is its convex hull to within the corner
             rounding, not an approximation of a complex shape. The swap is not about fit, it is
             about CONTACT COUNT -- mesh-vs-box yields a single point, and one point cannot hold a
             squeezed object, so the jaws sank into whatever they closed on. See the header. -->
        <geom name="gripper_left_finger" type="box" pos="0 -0.01331 0.0171"
          size="0.010 0.0022 0.010" group="3" mass="0" density="0" condim="4" solimp="2 1 0.01"
          solref="0.01 1" friction="6 0.5 0.1"/>
      </body>
      <body name="gripper_right_finger" pos="0 0 0.054" quat="1 0 0 0">
        <inertial pos="0.0 0.01 0.01" quat="1 0 0 0" mass="0.024"
          diaginertia="1.961e-6 2.274e-6 1.238e-6"/>
        <joint name="gripper_right_finger" pos="0 0 0" axis="0 1 0" type="slide" limited="true"
          range="1e-5 0.0081" damping="1" armature="0.1" frictionloss="0.4"/>
        <geom name="gripper_right_finger_visual" type="mesh" mesh="gripper_lite_right_finger_wide"
          material="black" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"/>
        <geom name="gripper_right_finger_base" type="mesh" mesh="gripper_lite_right_finger_wide_base"
          material="black" group="3" mass="0" density="0" condim="1" solimp="2 1 0.01"
          solref="0.01 1" friction="5 0.5 0.1"/>
        <geom name="gripper_right_finger" type="box" pos="0 0.01331 0.0171"
          size="0.010 0.0022 0.010" group="3" mass="0" density="0" condim="4" solimp="2 1 0.01"
          solref="0.01 1" friction="5 0.5 0.1"/>
      </body>
      <!-- End effector reference point is in the centre at the tip of the grippers. Upstream names
           it `end_effector`; renamed `grasp_site` here, see the header. -->
      <site name="grasp_site" pos="0 0 0.0811" size="0.001" rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 0.3" group="4"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- see the narrow sibling's header for the full account: detached from the jointed link6 the
       fingers' hulls sink into gripper_lite_body_c under a 659 N phantom clamp, because MuJoCo's
       filterparent does not treat a world-welded body as a parent. -->
  <contact>
    <exclude body1="gripper_body" body2="gripper_left_finger"/>
    <exclude body1="gripper_body" body2="gripper_right_finger"/>
  </contact>

  <actuator>
    <position name="gripper" joint="gripper_left_finger" ctrlrange="-0.0081 -1e-5"
      kp="500000" dampratio="1" forcerange="-10 10"/>
  </actuator>

  <equality>
    <joint joint1="gripper_left_finger" joint2="gripper_right_finger" polycoef="0 -1 0 0 0"/>
  </equality>
</mujoco>
